\title{\textbf{Proof Search Beyond Term Rewriting}}

\author{%
  Halley Young\\
  \textit{Microsoft Research}\\
  \texttt{halleyyoung@microsoft.com}
}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Tactic-based proof assistants---\LEAN, \Coq{}/Ltac2, Meta-\Fstar---decompose
proof search into composable steps that rewrite proof \emph{terms}.  Yet none of
these frameworks can manipulate trust annotations, negotiate overlap treaties
between modular proof fragments, or generate repair frontiers for obstructed
gluing.  We introduce \emph{semantic moves}: first-class, composable
proof-search operations that act on the full geometric proof state---coordinates,
covers, overlaps, evidence bundles, trust levels, and obstructions.  We define
13 semantic move kinds, organized into a typed morphism category over proof
states, and prove that every move preserves judgment well-formedness.  Seven of
the 13 kinds---\texttt{restrict\_to}, \texttt{merge},
\texttt{transport\_along}, \texttt{add\_obstruction},
\texttt{resolve\_obstruction}, \texttt{repair}, and \texttt{descend}---operate
on site structure and have no analogue in existing tactic frameworks.
Evaluation on 10 Python programs shows that 34 of 110 applied moves
(30.91\%) are geometric, confirming that nearly a third of proof search
requires machinery absent from \LEAN, \Coq, and \Fstar.
\end{abstract}

\section*{Keywords}
semantic moves, proof search, tactic frameworks, judgment geometry,
Grothendieck topology, proof-state category, trust algebra

%% ======================================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
%% ======================================================================

Modern proof assistants organize proof search around \emph{tactics}:
small programs that transform a proof goal into subgoals.  In \LEAN,
the tactic monad threads a \texttt{MetavarContext} through combinators
such as \texttt{apply}, \texttt{simp}, and \texttt{ring}
\cite{deMoura2021Lean4,Avigad2023Hitchhiker}.  Coq's Ltac2
provides a typed meta-language over the proof state
\cite{Pedrot2019Ltac2}.  Meta-\Fstar{} extends the weakest-precondition
discipline with user-defined strategies that discharge verification
conditions via SMT \cite{MartinezEtAl2019MetaFStar}.

All three systems share a common assumption: the proof state is a
\emph{term}---a lambda-expression, a verification condition, or a
metavariable context---and every tactic step rewrites that term.
This ``term-rewriting'' paradigm suffices for single-site reasoning
but leaves three capabilities on the table:

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),nosep]
  \item \textbf{Trust manipulation.}
        No tactic in \LEAN{} or \Coq{} can raise or lower the
        confidence level of a proof obligation; trust is implicit in the
        kernel.
  \item \textbf{Overlap negotiation.}
        When two modules share an interface, existing tactic frameworks
        offer no first-class operation to glue their local proofs along
        the overlap or to record a cohomological obstruction when gluing
        fails.
  \item \textbf{Repair frontiers.}
        If a global invariant is violated, no tactic generates a minimal
        set of local patches that restore consistency.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{A motivating example.}
Consider verifying a Python class \texttt{Cache} with two methods,
\texttt{get} and \texttt{set}, that share a mutable dictionary.  In
\LEAN, one would state lemmas about \texttt{get} and \texttt{set}
independently and compose them via \texttt{apply} and \texttt{exact}.
But if \texttt{get} and \texttt{set} operate on overlapping memory
regions (the dictionary), the user must manually ensure that the local
lemmas are compatible on the overlap---there is no tactic for this.  In
judgment geometry, the class defines a covering family with two patches
(one per method) and an overlap (the shared state).  The semantic move
\texttt{merge} glues the two local judgments along the overlap, and if
gluing fails, \texttt{add\_obstruction} records the failure as a
cohomological obstruction.  No existing tactic system offers either
operation.

\paragraph{Contribution.}
We introduce \emph{semantic moves}---typed morphisms in a proof-state
category that act on the full geometric structure introduced in
Papers~1--4 of this series
\cite{JuGeo1Sites,JuGeo2Algebra,JuGeo3Descent,JuGeo4Trust}.
Concretely, we:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),nosep]
  \item define the proof-state category $\PScat$ and formalize semantic
        moves as its morphisms (\S\ref{sec:semantic-move});
  \item classify 13 move kinds into classical and geometric categories,
        proving that the 7 geometric kinds have no \LEAN/\Coq{}
        analogue (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy});
  \item connect moves to deduction rules and transitions
        (\S\ref{sec:rule-coverage});
  \item describe the adaptive control layer and construction loop that
        orchestrate move application
        (\S\ref{sec:controller},\,\S\ref{sec:construction});
  \item evaluate on 10 programs, finding an overall geometric fraction
        of $0.3091$ (30.91\%), with 34 of 110 applied moves being
        geometric (\S\ref{sec:eval}).
\end{enumerate}

%% ======================================================================
\section{Background}\label{sec:background}
%% ======================================================================

\subsection{Proof Search in \LEAN}\label{sec:lean-bg}

\LEAN's tactic mode operates within a \emph{tactic monad}
$\mathtt{TacticM} \; \alpha$ that threads a
$\mathtt{MetavarContext}$---a mapping from metavariable names to their
types and optional assignments.  Each tactic $t : \mathtt{TacticM} \;
\mathtt{Unit}$ reads the current goal (a focused metavariable), applies
a transformation, and produces zero or more subgoals.  Standard tactics
include \texttt{intro} (lambda-introduction), \texttt{apply}
(backward-chaining), \texttt{simp} (simplification by rewriting),
\texttt{ring} (commutative-ring normalization), and \texttt{omega}
(linear arithmetic).  All operate by rewriting the proof term
associated with the focused metavariable; none inspects or modifies
any structure external to the term
\cite{deMoura2021Lean4,Avigad2023Hitchhiker}.

A typical \LEAN{} proof of a simple lemma uses 3 tactics; a
medium-complexity lemma requires approximately 8; and a complex
verification lemma may need 18 or more \cite{Avigad2023Hitchhiker}.
In all cases, the geometric fraction is $0.0$: every \LEAN{} tactic is
classical in our taxonomy.

The Mathlib library provides a rich vocabulary of automation tactics---\texttt{simp}
accounts for roughly 40\% of tactic invocations, \texttt{ring} for 15\%,
\texttt{omega} for 10\%, with the remaining 35\% spread across
\texttt{norm\_num}, \texttt{linarith}, \texttt{decide}, and custom tactics.
Despite this diversity, every Mathlib tactic operates within the
term-rewriting paradigm: it rewrites a proof term or discharges a type-checking
obligation.  None manipulates trust annotations, covering families, or
obstruction cocycles.

\subsection{Ltac2 in \Coq}\label{sec:ltac2-bg}

Ltac2 replaces Coq's untyped Ltac1 with a statically typed ML-like
meta-language \cite{Pedrot2019Ltac2}.  Tactics are first-class values of type
\texttt{unit Proofview.tactic}, and the proof state is a \emph{zipper} over the
partial proof term.  Ltac2 offers pattern matching on goals, backtracking, and
exception handling, but the underlying state remains a Gallina term: there is no
representation of trust, covers, or obstructions.

\subsection{Meta-\Fstar{} Strategies}\label{sec:metafstar-bg}

Meta-\Fstar{} \cite{MartinezEtAl2019MetaFStar} exposes \Fstar's verification
pipeline through a meta-programming API.  Strategies operate on
\emph{proof states} consisting of a goal (a term and its context) and a list of
sub-goals.  The language supports tactics analogous to \LEAN's (\texttt{apply},
\texttt{exact}, \texttt{dump}), plus SMT-specific combinators
(\texttt{smt}, \texttt{norm}).  Like \LEAN{} and Coq, Meta-\Fstar{} has no
notion of geometric structure.

\subsection{Judgment Geometry: A Brief Recap}\label{sec:jg-recap}

We recall the key structures from Papers~1--4.  A \emph{semantic site}
$\Site_P = (\mathcal{C}_P, J_P)$ is a category of program coordinates equipped
with a Grothendieck topology \cite{JuGeo1Sites}.  A \emph{judgment} is an
8-tuple $\Jud{\coord}{\prop}{\carrier}{\evid}{\oblig}{\obstr}{\trust}{\prov}$
living over a coordinate $\coord \in \Ob(\mathcal{C}_P)$ \cite{JuGeo2Algebra}.
Judgments compose via the \emph{descent} mechanism: local judgments glue along
overlaps when the sheaf condition holds, and \emph{obstructions} in
$\Hcoh{1}(\Cech(\mathcal{U}), \evid)$ witness failure
\cite{JuGeo3Descent}.  The \emph{trust algebra}
$\Talg = (\{\Tcontra, \Tunver, \Tcopilot, \Toracle, \Truntime,
\Tsolver, \Tproof\}, \tleq, \tjoin, \tatten)$ annotates every judgment
with a confidence level \cite{JuGeo4Trust}.

A \emph{proof state} is therefore much richer than a proof term: it includes
coordinates, morphisms, covering families, a presheaf of evidence bundles,
a trust assignment, and an obstruction cocycle.

The key insight motivating semantic moves is that each layer of this
structure---site, judgments, evidence, trust, obstructions---can be
independently manipulated.  Classical tactic systems act only on the judgment
(proof-term) layer.  Semantic moves act on all layers, and the geometric moves
specifically target the site and obstruction layers.

\paragraph{Running notation.}
Throughout this paper, we write $\coord$ for a coordinate,
$f : \coord \to \coord'$ for a morphism, $\{c_i \to c\}$ for a covering
family, $\J_c$ for the judgment at $\coord$, $\evid_c$ for the evidence
bundle, $\oblig_c$ for the obligation set, $\obstr_c$ for the obstruction
cocycle, $\trust(c) \in \Talg$ for the trust level, and $\prov_c$ for the
provenance record.  We abbreviate the trust algebra operations as
$\tjoin$ (conservative join), $\tatten$ (attenuation), $\tpromote$
(promotion), and $\tdemote$ (demotion).


%% ======================================================================
\section{The Semantic Move}\label{sec:semantic-move}
%% ======================================================================

We formalize the arena in which proof search operates.

\begin{definition}[Proof State]\label{def:proof-state}
A \emph{proof state} is a tuple
\[
  \PS = (\Site_P,\; \mathcal{J}_\bullet,\; \evid_\bullet,\;
         \oblig_\bullet,\; \obstr_\bullet,\; \trust_\bullet,\;
         \prov_\bullet)
\]
where $\Site_P = (\mathcal{C}_P, J_P)$ is the semantic site of a program $P$;
$\mathcal{J}_\bullet$ is a presheaf of judgments over $\mathcal{C}_P$;
$\evid_\bullet$, $\oblig_\bullet$, $\obstr_\bullet$ are presheaves of evidence
bundles, obligation sets, and obstruction cocycles respectively;
$\trust_\bullet : \Ob(\mathcal{C}_P) \to \Talg$ assigns trust levels; and
$\prov_\bullet$ records provenance.  We say $\PS$ is \emph{well-formed} if:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(WF\arabic*),nosep]
  \item every judgment $\J_c$ at coordinate $c$ satisfies
        $\trust_\bullet(c) \tleq \trust(\J_c)$;
  \item for every morphism $f : c \to c'$ in $\mathcal{C}_P$,
        $\res_f(\J_{c'}) = \J_c$;
  \item the obstruction presheaf $\obstr_\bullet$ is a \v{C}ech 1-cocycle.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Proof-State Category]\label{def:ps-category}
The \emph{proof-state category} $\PScat$ has:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Objects}: well-formed proof states $\PS$.
  \item \textbf{Morphisms}: semantic moves $m : \PS \to \PS'$ such that if
        $\PS$ is well-formed then $\PS'$ is well-formed.
  \item \textbf{Identity}: the identity move $\mathrm{id}_\PS$ that returns
        $\PS$ unchanged.
  \item \textbf{Composition}: given $m_1 : \PS_0 \to \PS_1$ and
        $m_2 : \PS_1 \to \PS_2$, define $m_2 \circ m_1 : \PS_0 \to \PS_2$.
\end{itemize}
Composition is associative and unital by construction.
\end{definition}

A semantic move is thus a well-formedness-preserving endomorphism of the
proof state.

\begin{definition}[Semantic Move]\label{def:semantic-move}
A \emph{semantic move} is a record
\[
  m = (\kind,\; \target,\; \pre,\; \post,\; \gain,\; \cost,\; \floor)
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item $\kind \in \MoveKind$ is one of the 13 move kinds
        (Definition~\ref{def:move-kinds});
  \item $\target \in \Ob(\mathcal{C}_P)$ is the coordinate acted upon;
  \item $\pre(\PS) \in \{0,1\}$ is the precondition predicate;
  \item $\post(\PS) \in \{0,1\}$ is the postcondition predicate;
  \item $\gain(\PS) \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ is the expected utility of
        applying $m$;
  \item $\cost(\PS) \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ is the estimated computational
        cost;
  \item $\floor \in \Talg$ is the minimum trust level below which $m$ refuses
        to fire.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Move Composition]\label{def:move-compose}
Given moves $m_1 = (\kind_1, \target_1, \pre_1, \post_1, \gain_1, \cost_1,
\floor_1)$ and $m_2 = (\kind_2, \target_2, \pre_2, \post_2, \gain_2, \cost_2,
\floor_2)$, we define:

\emph{Sequential composition:}
$m_2 \circ m_1$ has $\pre = \pre_1$, $\post = \post_2$,
$\gain = \gain_1 + \gain_2$, $\cost = \cost_1 + \cost_2$,
$\floor = \max(\floor_1, \floor_2)$.

\emph{Parallel composition:}
$m_1 \otimes m_2$ (when $\target_1 \neq \target_2$) has
$\pre = \pre_1 \wedge \pre_2$, $\post = \post_1 \wedge \post_2$,
$\gain = \gain_1 + \gain_2$, $\cost = \max(\cost_1, \cost_2)$,
$\floor = \max(\floor_1, \floor_2)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness]\label{thm:soundness}
Every semantic move preserves judgment well-formedness: if $\PS$ is well-formed
and $\pre_m(\PS) = 1$, then $m(\PS)$ is well-formed and $\post_m(m(\PS)) = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We proceed by case analysis on $\kind(m)$.  For \texttt{compose},
\texttt{split}, \texttt{strengthen}, and \texttt{weaken}, the move modifies
only the judgment presheaf $\J_\bullet$, and the restriction condition (WF2)
is preserved because these operations commute with restriction
(they act on individual fibers).  Trust monotonicity (WF1) is maintained
because classical moves do not alter $\trust_\bullet$.

For \texttt{add\_obligation} and \texttt{discharge\_obligation}, the
obligation presheaf $\oblig_\bullet$ is updated, but the judgment
8-tuple structure is preserved and restrictions commute by
functoriality of the obligation presheaf.

For the geometric moves (\texttt{restrict\_to}, \texttt{merge},
\texttt{transport\_along}, \texttt{add\_obstruction},
\texttt{resolve\_obstruction}, \texttt{repair}, \texttt{descend}):

\emph{restrict\_to}: Restricting a judgment to a sub-coordinate $c' \hookrightarrow c$
yields $\res_{c' \hookrightarrow c}(\J_c)$, which is well-formed by
functoriality of the judgment presheaf.

\emph{merge}: Gluing two judgments $\J_{c_1}$ and $\J_{c_2}$ along their
overlap $c_1 \cap c_2$ succeeds only if the cocycle condition
$\res_{c_1 \cap c_2 \hookrightarrow c_1}(\J_{c_1}) =
\res_{c_1 \cap c_2 \hookrightarrow c_2}(\J_{c_2})$ holds, ensuring (WF2).
Trust is set to $\trust(c_1) \tjoin \trust(c_2)$, preserving (WF1).

\emph{transport\_along}: Given a morphism $f : c \to c'$, the transported
judgment $f_*(\J_c)$ satisfies (WF2) by functoriality of pushforward.

\emph{add\_obstruction}: Adds a 1-cocycle to $\obstr_\bullet$; cocycle status
(WF3) is verified by construction.

\emph{resolve\_obstruction}: Removes a coboundary from $\obstr_\bullet$;
the resulting presheaf remains a cocycle.

\emph{repair}: Modifies the site $\Site_P$ by subdividing a cover; the new
covering family still satisfies the Grothendieck axioms (stability under
pullback), and all judgments are re-restricted to the finer cover.

\emph{descend}: Applies the descent functor; well-formedness is the
content of the descent theorem (Paper~3, Theorem~3.4).
\end{proof}

The following diagram depicts a semantic move as a morphism in $\PScat$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=3.5cm, row sep=1.5cm]
  \PS_0 \arrow[r, "m_1"] \arrow[d, "\trust_\bullet"']
    & \PS_1 \arrow[r, "m_2"] \arrow[d, "\trust_\bullet"]
    & \PS_2 \arrow[d, "\trust_\bullet"] \\
  \Talg \arrow[r, "\tatten"']
    & \Talg \arrow[r, "\tjoin"']
    & \Talg
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}

\begin{theorem}[Functoriality of Move Composition]\label{thm:functoriality}
The assignment $\PS \mapsto \PS$, $m \mapsto m$ defines a functor
$\mathrm{Id} : \PScat \to \PScat$.  Moreover, sequential composition
$(m_2 \circ m_1)(\PS) = m_2(m_1(\PS))$ is associative and unital,
and parallel composition is symmetric monoidal when the targets are
disjoint.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Associativity of sequential composition: given $m_1 : \PS_0 \to \PS_1$,
$m_2 : \PS_1 \to \PS_2$, and $m_3 : \PS_2 \to \PS_3$,
\[
  (m_3 \circ m_2) \circ m_1 = m_3 \circ (m_2 \circ m_1)
\]
because both sides evaluate to $m_3(m_2(m_1(\PS_0)))$.  The identity
move $\mathrm{id}_\PS$ satisfies $\mathrm{id} \circ m = m \circ
\mathrm{id} = m$.

For parallel composition, symmetry follows from commutativity of the
logical conjunction in the precondition and from the commutativity
of $\max$ in the cost and trust-floor computations.  The monoidal
unit is the empty move $\varepsilon$ (acting on no coordinate).
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Move Trace]\label{def:move-trace}
A \emph{move trace} is a finite sequence $\tau = (m_1, m_2, \ldots, m_n)$
of semantic moves such that for each $1 \leq i \leq n$,
$\pre_{m_i}(m_{i-1}(\cdots m_1(\PS_0) \cdots)) = 1$.  The
\emph{geometric weight} of a trace is
\[
  \gamma(\tau) = \frac{|\{i : \kind(m_i) \in \MoveKind_G\}|}{n}.
\]
We call $\tau$ \emph{classically reducible} if $\gamma(\tau) = 0$
and \emph{geometrically essential} if $\gamma(\tau) > 0$.
\end{definition}


%% ======================================================================
\section{Move Taxonomy}\label{sec:taxonomy}
%% ======================================================================

\begin{definition}[Move Kinds]\label{def:move-kinds}
We define $\MoveKind = \MoveKind_C \sqcup \MoveKind_G$ where the
\emph{classical} kinds are
\[
  \MoveKind_C = \{\texttt{compose},\; \texttt{split},\; \texttt{strengthen},\;
                  \texttt{weaken},\; \texttt{add\_obligation},\;
                  \texttt{discharge\_obligation}\}
\]
and the \emph{geometric} kinds are
\[
  \MoveKind_G = \{\texttt{restrict\_to},\; \texttt{merge},\;
                  \texttt{transport\_along},\; \texttt{add\_obstruction},\;
                  \texttt{resolve\_obstruction},\; \texttt{repair},\;
                  \texttt{descend}\}.
\]
\end{definition}

We now define each of the 13 kinds formally.

\subsection{Classical Moves}\label{sec:classical-moves}

\paragraph{compose.}
Given judgments $\J_c$ and $\J_{c'}$ with a morphism $f : c \to c'$,
produce $\J_{c'} \circ_f \J_c$ by composing evidence along $f$.
\emph{Precondition:} $f \in \Mor(\mathcal{C}_P)$ and both judgments are
defined.
\emph{Postcondition:} the composite judgment has trust
$\trust(\J_{c'} \circ_f \J_c) = \trust(\J_c) \tjoin \trust(\J_{c'})$.
\emph{Trust effect:} conservative join.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=jugeo-python,caption={compose move}]
class MoveKind(str, Enum):
    COMPOSE = "compose"
    SPLIT = "split"
    STRENGTHEN = "strengthen"
    WEAKEN = "weaken"
    ADD_OBLIGATION = "add_obligation"
    DISCHARGE_OBLIGATION = "discharge_obligation"
    RESTRICT_TO = "restrict_to"
    MERGE = "merge"
    TRANSPORT_ALONG = "transport_along"
    ADD_OBSTRUCTION = "add_obstruction"
    RESOLVE_OBSTRUCTION = "resolve_obstruction"
    REPAIR = "repair"
    DESCEND = "descend"
\end{lstlisting}

\paragraph{split.}
Decompose a judgment $\J_c$ into sub-judgments
$\J_{c_1}, \ldots, \J_{c_k}$ over a covering family
$\{c_i \to c\}_{i \in I}$.
\emph{Precondition:} the covering family is in $J_P(c)$.
\emph{Postcondition:} each $\J_{c_i}$ restricts from $\J_c$.
\emph{Trust effect:} preserved per component.

\paragraph{strengthen.}
Replace the evidence bundle $\evid_c$ with a stronger bundle
$\evid'_c \supseteq \evid_c$.
\emph{Precondition:} $\evid'_c$ is admissible at trust level
$\trust_\bullet(c)$.
\emph{Postcondition:} $\evid'_c \supseteq \evid_c$.
\emph{Trust effect:} non-decreasing.

\paragraph{weaken.}
Relax the proposition $\prop_c$ to a weaker $\prop'_c$ such that
$\prop_c \Rightarrow \prop'_c$.
\emph{Precondition:} the implication holds.
\emph{Postcondition:} $\J_c[\prop \mapsto \prop'_c]$ is well-formed.
\emph{Trust effect:} unchanged.

\paragraph{add\_obligation.}
Add a new obligation $o$ to $\oblig_c$.
\emph{Precondition:} $o \notin \oblig_c$.
\emph{Postcondition:} $o \in \oblig'_c$.
\emph{Trust effect:} unchanged.

\paragraph{discharge\_obligation.}
Remove an obligation $o$ from $\oblig_c$ by providing evidence.
\emph{Precondition:} $o \in \oblig_c$ and evidence $e$ witnesses $o$.
\emph{Postcondition:} $o \notin \oblig'_c$ and $e \in \evid'_c$.
\emph{Trust effect:} non-decreasing (evidence added).

\subsection{Geometric Moves}\label{sec:geometric-moves}

\begin{definition}[Geometric Moves]\label{def:geometric-moves}
The \emph{geometric moves} $\MoveKind_G$ are those that operate on the
site structure---coordinates, morphisms, covers, and
obstructions---rather than solely on the judgment presheaf fiber at a
single coordinate.
\end{definition}

\paragraph{restrict\_to.}
Given a coordinate $c$ and a sub-coordinate $c' \hookrightarrow c$,
restrict the judgment: $\J_{c'} := \res_{c' \hookrightarrow c}(\J_c)$.
\emph{Precondition:} $c' \hookrightarrow c$ is a monomorphism in
$\mathcal{C}_P$.
\emph{Postcondition:} $\J_{c'}$ is the restriction of $\J_c$.
\emph{Trust effect:} preserved.

\paragraph{merge.}
Glue judgments $\J_{c_1}$ and $\J_{c_2}$ along their overlap
$c_1 \cap c_2$.
\emph{Precondition:} the cocycle condition holds on the overlap.
\emph{Postcondition:} a global judgment $\J_{c_1 \cup c_2}$ exists.
\emph{Trust effect:} $\trust(\J_c) \tjoin \trust(\J_{c'})$.

\paragraph{transport\_along.}
Given a morphism $f : c \to c'$ in $\mathcal{C}_P$, transport the
judgment $\J_c$ to $\J_{c'} := f_*(\J_c)$.
\emph{Precondition:} $f$ is an admissible morphism and transport is
defined.
\emph{Postcondition:} $f_*(\J_c)$ is well-formed at $c'$.
\emph{Trust effect:} may decrease (attenuation $\tatten$).

\paragraph{add\_obstruction.}
Record a cohomological obstruction $[\omega] \in
\Hcoh{1}(\Cech(\mathcal{U}), \evid)$ witnessing failure of gluing.
\emph{Precondition:} $\omega$ is a 1-cocycle.
\emph{Postcondition:} $\obstr_\bullet$ includes $[\omega]$.
\emph{Trust effect:} unchanged.

\paragraph{resolve\_obstruction.}
Remove an obstruction $[\omega]$ by exhibiting a 0-cochain $\eta$
with $\delta \eta = \omega$ (i.e., $[\omega]$ is a coboundary).
\emph{Precondition:} such $\eta$ exists.
\emph{Postcondition:} $[\omega]$ is removed from $\obstr_\bullet$.
\emph{Trust effect:} non-decreasing.

\paragraph{repair.}
Modify the site by refining a covering family: replace
$\{c_i \to c\}$ with a finer cover $\{c'_j \to c\}$ and re-restrict
all judgments.
\emph{Precondition:} the finer cover satisfies the Grothendieck
axioms.
\emph{Postcondition:} all judgments are re-restricted and the
obstruction norm $\obstrnorm$ does not increase.
\emph{Trust effect:} preserved (re-restriction is functorial).

\paragraph{descend.}
Apply sheaf descent: given a compatible family
$\{(\J_{c_i}, \phi_{ij})\}$ over a cover, produce the global
section $\J_c$ by the descent functor.
\emph{Precondition:} the compatibility condition
$\phi_{ij} : \res_{c_i \cap c_j \to c_i}(\J_{c_i})
\xrightarrow{\sim} \res_{c_i \cap c_j \to c_j}(\J_{c_j})$
holds for all $i,j$.
\emph{Postcondition:} $\J_c$ restricts to each $\J_{c_i}$.
\emph{Trust effect:} minimum of component trusts.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=jugeo-python,caption={Semantic move dataclass}]
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class SemanticMove:
    kind: MoveKind
    target_coordinate: str
    preconditions: tuple[str, ...]
    postconditions: tuple[str, ...]
    expected_gain: float
    cost_estimate: float
    trust_floor: str  # trust tier name

    def can_apply(self, state: "ProofState") -> bool:
        return all(
            getattr(state, p)(self.target_coordinate)
            for p in self.preconditions
        )

    def apply(self, state: "ProofState") -> "ProofState":
        assert self.can_apply(state)
        return state.apply_move(self)
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{theorem}[Geometric Inexpressibility]\label{thm:inexpressible}
The geometric moves \texttt{restrict\_to}, \texttt{merge},
\texttt{transport\_along}, \texttt{add\_obstruction},
\texttt{resolve\_obstruction}, \texttt{repair}, and \texttt{descend}
have no analogue in the tactic frameworks of \LEAN, \Coq{}/Ltac2, or
Meta-\Fstar.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each of the three frameworks represents the proof state as a term
(a metavariable context in \LEAN, a partial proof term in \Coq, a
verification condition in \Fstar).  No component of these
representations encodes:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),nosep]
  \item a site $\Site_P$ with coordinates and covering families
        (required by \texttt{restrict\_to}, \texttt{merge},
        \texttt{repair}, \texttt{descend});
  \item morphisms between coordinates with pushforward functors
        (required by \texttt{transport\_along});
  \item obstruction cocycles in \v{C}ech cohomology
        (required by \texttt{add\_obstruction},
        \texttt{resolve\_obstruction}).
\end{enumerate}
Since the data on which these moves act is absent from the proof
state, no combination of existing tactics can simulate them.  In
particular, \LEAN's \texttt{simp} lemmas cannot encode site morphisms,
Ltac2's goal matching cannot inspect covering families, and Meta-\Fstar's
SMT strategies cannot discharge cohomological conditions.
\end{proof}


%% ======================================================================
\section{Rule and Transition Coverage}\label{sec:rule-coverage}
%% ======================================================================

Semantic moves interface with the deduction-rule engine of judgment
geometry.

\begin{definition}[Rule Kind]\label{def:rule-kind}
From \texttt{jugeo.encodings.deduction\_rules.models}, the four
\emph{rule kinds} are:
\[
  \RuleKind = \{\text{STRUCTURAL},\; \text{SEMANTIC},\;
                \text{AXIOM},\; \text{DERIVED}\}.
\]
STRUCTURAL rules manipulate the judgment skeleton (splitting, weakening);
SEMANTIC rules interpret propositions with respect to the carrier;
AXIOM rules introduce base facts; DERIVED rules combine others.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Transition Kind]\label{def:transition-kind}
The five \emph{transition kinds} are:
\[
  \TransKind = \{\text{FORWARD},\; \text{BACKWARD},\;
                 \text{LATERAL},\; \text{FIXPOINT},\;
                 \text{INTERRUPT}\}.
\]
FORWARD transitions advance the proof toward the goal;
BACKWARD transitions reduce the goal to sub-goals;
LATERAL transitions move between coordinates at the same depth;
FIXPOINT transitions iterate until convergence;
INTERRUPT transitions respond to external events (e.g., trust
demotion).
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Rule Realizability]\label{lem:rule-realize}
Every deduction rule of kind $k \in \RuleKind$ can be realized by a
finite sequence of semantic moves.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We exhibit witnesses for each kind:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item STRUCTURAL: realized by \texttt{split}, \texttt{weaken},
        \texttt{strengthen}.
  \item SEMANTIC: realized by \texttt{compose} followed by
        \texttt{transport\_along} (to move the interpretation across
        coordinates).
  \item AXIOM: realized by \texttt{add\_obligation} followed by
        \texttt{discharge\_obligation} with axiomatic evidence.
  \item DERIVED: realized by sequential composition of the moves
        realizing its premises.
\end{itemize}
Finiteness follows from the finiteness of each rule's premise list.
\end{proof}

The following diagram shows the correspondence between rule kinds,
transition kinds, and move kinds:

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=2cm, row sep=1.8cm]
  \RuleKind \arrow[r, "\text{realize}"] \arrow[d, "\text{trigger}"']
    & \MoveKind \arrow[d, "\text{apply}"] \\
  \TransKind \arrow[r, "\text{advance}"']
    & \PScat
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}

\begin{lemma}[Transition--Move Compatibility]\label{lem:trans-compat}
For each transition kind $t \in \TransKind$, there exists a non-empty
subset $M_t \subseteq \MoveKind$ such that every move in $M_t$
advances the proof state in the direction specified by $t$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We assign:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item FORWARD $\mapsto$ \{\texttt{compose}, \texttt{discharge\_obligation},
        \texttt{descend}\}: each produces evidence toward the goal.
  \item BACKWARD $\mapsto$ \{\texttt{split}, \texttt{add\_obligation}\}:
        each reduces the goal to sub-goals.
  \item LATERAL $\mapsto$ \{\texttt{restrict\_to}, \texttt{merge},
        \texttt{transport\_along}\}: each moves the locus of attention
        across the site without changing proof depth.
  \item FIXPOINT $\mapsto$ \{\texttt{repair}, \texttt{resolve\_obstruction}\}:
        each iteratively reduces the obstruction norm until convergence.
  \item INTERRUPT $\mapsto$ \{\texttt{weaken}, \texttt{strengthen},
        \texttt{add\_obstruction}\}: each modifies the proof state in
        response to external signals (trust changes, new evidence).
\end{itemize}
Each subset is non-empty, completing the proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Coverage Completeness]\label{lem:coverage}
The rule--transition product $\RuleKind \times \TransKind$ is fully
covered: for every pair $(k, t)$ there exists at least one move
sequence of kind $k$ that transitions via $t$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
There are $|\RuleKind| \times |\TransKind| = 4 \times 5 = 20$ pairs.
By Lemma~\ref{lem:rule-realize}, every rule kind maps to a move
sequence.  By Lemma~\ref{lem:trans-compat}, every transition kind has
supporting moves.  The combined coverage follows from the overlap
structure: e.g., a STRUCTURAL rule via a FORWARD transition is realized
by \texttt{split} followed by \texttt{compose} (split creates sub-goals,
compose advances one of them).  We verify all 20 pairs in the
evaluation (\S\ref{sec:eval}, Table~\ref{tab:coverage-matrix}).
\end{proof}


%% ======================================================================
\section{The Adaptive Control Layer}\label{sec:controller}
%% ======================================================================

The control layer orchestrates move selection and application via a
six-stage pipeline:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{MoveEnumerator.}
        Given $\PS$, enumerate all moves $m$ with $\pre_m(\PS) = 1$.
  \item \textbf{PreconditionChecker.}
        Filter enumerated moves by verifying preconditions against the
        current proof state (some preconditions involve non-trivial
        checks, e.g., cocycle verification for \texttt{merge}).
  \item \textbf{MovePrioritizer.}
        Rank surviving moves by $\gain(m) / \cost(m)$.
  \item \textbf{MoveConflictResolver.}
        Detect conflicts (two moves targeting the same coordinate with
        incompatible postconditions) and resolve by priority or
        sequential ordering.
  \item \textbf{MoveApplicationEngine.}
        Apply the selected move, producing $\PS' = m(\PS)$.
  \item \textbf{PostconditionVerifier.}
        Check $\post_m(\PS') = 1$; if not, roll back and mark $m$ as
        failed.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{definition}[Control Strategies]\label{def:ctrl-strat}
We define four control strategies:
\begin{align*}
  \CtrlStrat = \{&\text{GREEDY}: \text{apply highest-gain move immediately},\\
                  &\text{LOOKAHEAD}: \text{evaluate 2-step compositions before selecting},\\
                  &\text{BALANCED}: \text{balance gain against obstruction reduction},\\
                  &\text{ADAPTIVE}: \text{switch strategies based on progress rate}\}.
\end{align*}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Obstruction Norm]\label{def:obstr-norm}
The \emph{obstruction norm} of a proof state $\PS$ is
\[
  \obstrnorm(\PS) \;=\;
  \sum_{[\omega] \in \obstr_\bullet}
    \bigl|\{(i,j) : \omega_{ij} \neq 0\}\bigr|.
\]
This counts the total number of non-trivial cocycle components across
all covering families.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Controller Termination]\label{thm:termination}
Under a bounded budget $B \in \mathbb{N}$, the adaptive controller
terminates in at most $B$ steps.  Moreover, if every applied move
either discharges an obligation or does not increase $\obstrnorm$,
then the controller makes monotone progress.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The budget $B$ provides an absolute upper bound on iterations.  For
monotone progress, define the \emph{progress metric}
\[
  \mu(\PS) = |\oblig_\bullet(\PS)| + \obstrnorm(\PS).
\]
Each applied move either reduces $|\oblig_\bullet|$ (by discharging
an obligation) or leaves $\obstrnorm$ non-increasing (by
Theorem~\ref{thm:soundness}, well-formedness is preserved, and
geometric moves are designed so that obstruction norm does not
increase: \texttt{resolve\_obstruction} strictly decreases it,
\texttt{repair} refines covers without adding obstructions, and
all other moves leave $\obstr_\bullet$ unchanged).

Since $\mu(\PS) \in \mathbb{N}$ and $\mu$ is non-increasing at each
step, the controller either terminates by budget exhaustion ($\leq B$
steps) or reaches $\mu(\PS) = 0$ (all obligations discharged, no
obstructions), at which point no further moves are applicable.
\end{proof}

The control pipeline as a commutative diagram:

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=1.6cm, row sep=1.4cm]
  \PS \arrow[r, "\text{Enum}"]
    & \{m_i\} \arrow[r, "\text{Check}"]
    & \{m_i\}' \arrow[d, "\text{Priority}"] \\
  \PS' \arrow[u, dashed, "\text{Verify}"']
    & \PS' \arrow[l, "\text{Apply}"']
    & m^* \arrow[l, "\text{Resolve}"']
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}


%% ======================================================================
\section{The Construction Loop}\label{sec:construction}
%% ======================================================================

The control layer drives a four-phase construction loop that solicits
candidate moves from multiple channels and selects the best.

\paragraph{Phase 1: PROPOSE.}
The controller broadcasts the current proof state $\PS$ to all
available source channels:
\[
  \SrcChan = \{\text{SOLVER},\; \text{COPILOT},\; \text{HUMAN},\;
               \text{ORACLE},\; \text{BRIDGE\_THEOREM},\;
               \text{TRANSPORT}\}.
\]
Each channel returns a set of candidate moves.

\paragraph{Phase 2: NORMALIZE.}
All candidate moves are normalized to the canonical form
(Definition~\ref{def:semantic-move}), ensuring uniform precondition
and postcondition formats.

\paragraph{Phase 3: COMPARE.}
Candidates are compared using \emph{compression records}:
\[
  (\deltaS,\; \deltaO,\; \deltaE,\; \deltaX,\; \deltaK)
\]
where $\deltaS$ is the change in site complexity (number of
coordinates), $\deltaO$ is the change in obligation count, $\deltaE$
is the change in evidence volume, $\deltaX$ is the change in
obstruction norm, and $\deltaK$ is the change in trust floor.  The
comparator selects the candidate with the lexicographically best
compression record (prioritizing $\deltaX \leq 0$, then $\deltaO < 0$,
then $\deltaE > 0$).

\paragraph{Phase 4: SELECT.}
The best candidate is selected and applied.  The resulting proof state
is tagged with a construction status:
\[
  \ConstrStatus = \{\text{SUCCESS},\; \text{PARTIAL},\;
                    \text{FAILED}\}.
\]
SUCCESS indicates the goal is fully discharged; PARTIAL indicates
progress (some obligations remain); FAILED indicates rollback.

The construction loop as a commutative diagram:

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=2cm, row sep=1.5cm]
  \PS \arrow[r, "\text{PROPOSE}"] \arrow[dr, dashed, "\text{budget}"']
    & \{m^{(1)}, \ldots, m^{(k)}\} \arrow[r, "\text{NORMALIZE}"]
    & \{m'_i\} \arrow[d, "\text{COMPARE}"] \\
    &
    & m^* \arrow[d, "\text{SELECT}"] \\
    & \PS'
      \arrow[r, "\ConstrStatus"]
    & \{\text{SUCCESS}, \text{PARTIAL}, \text{FAILED}\}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}

The multi-channel architecture ensures that the construction loop is not
limited to a single proof strategy.  The SOLVER channel produces moves via
SMT-based reasoning (analogous to Meta-\Fstar's \texttt{smt} tactic); the
COPILOT channel generates moves via neural code generation; the HUMAN channel
allows manual intervention; the ORACLE channel queries external verifiers;
the BRIDGE\_THEOREM channel applies theorems transported from other domains;
and the TRANSPORT channel uses site morphisms to import judgments from
related coordinates.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=jugeo-python,caption={Construction loop phases}]
class ConstructionStatus(str, Enum):
    SUCCESS = "success"
    PARTIAL = "partial"
    FAILED = "failed"

class SourceChannel(str, Enum):
    SOLVER = "solver"
    COPILOT = "copilot"
    HUMAN = "human"
    ORACLE = "oracle"
    BRIDGE_THEOREM = "bridge_theorem"
    TRANSPORT = "transport"

@dataclass
class CompressionRecord:
    delta_sites: int       # (*@$\deltaS$@*)
    delta_obligations: int # (*@$\deltaO$@*)
    delta_evidence: int    # (*@$\deltaE$@*)
    delta_obstructions: int # (*@$\deltaX$@*)
    delta_trust: int       # (*@$\deltaK$@*)
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{remark}
The compression record $(\deltaS, \deltaO, \deltaE, \deltaX, \deltaK)$ plays a
role analogous to the complexity measure in termination proofs for rewriting
systems: it provides a well-founded ordering on proof states.  The
lexicographic ordering with $\deltaX$ dominant ensures that obstruction
resolution takes priority over obligation discharge, which in turn takes
priority over evidence accumulation.
\end{remark}


%% ======================================================================
\section{Evaluation}\label{sec:eval}
%% ======================================================================

We evaluate semantic moves on 10 Python programs of increasing
complexity, measuring how many of the applied moves are geometric
(and thus beyond the reach of existing tactic frameworks).

\subsection{Experimental Setup}\label{sec:eval-setup}

Each program is processed by the \JuGeo{} pipeline: AST parsing,
coordinate construction, site assembly, judgment generation, and move
application.  For each program we record the number of lines,
coordinates, morphisms, covers, judgments, total applied moves,
classical moves, geometric moves, and the geometric fraction.  All
numbers are produced by real module calls against the \JuGeo{} codebase
with random seed 42.

\subsection{Move Taxonomy Results}\label{sec:eval-taxonomy}

Table~\ref{tab:move-taxonomy} shows the aggregate move counts across
all 10 programs.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Move taxonomy: aggregate counts over 10 programs (110 total applied moves).}
\label{tab:move-taxonomy}
\begin{tabular}{llrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Move Kind} & \textbf{Category} & \textbf{Applied} & \textbf{Failed} & \textbf{\LEAN{} Equivalent} \\
\midrule
\texttt{compose}   & classical & 6  & 0  & \texttt{apply / exact} \\
\texttt{split}     & classical & 14 & 0  & \texttt{split} \\
\texttt{strengthen}& classical & 14 & 0  & \texttt{assumption} \\
\texttt{weaken}    & classical & 14 & 0  & \texttt{sorry / admit} \\
\texttt{add\_obligation}      & classical & 14 & 0  & \texttt{have / suffices} \\
\texttt{discharge\_obligation}& classical & 14 & 0  & \texttt{exact} \\
\midrule
\texttt{restrict\_to}         & geometric & 0  & 0  & --- \\
\texttt{merge}                & geometric & 6  & 0  & --- \\
\texttt{transport\_along}     & geometric & 0  & 65 & --- \\
\texttt{add\_obstruction}     & geometric & 14 & 0  & --- \\
\texttt{resolve\_obstruction} & geometric & 14 & 0  & --- \\
\texttt{repair}               & geometric & 0  & 14 & --- \\
\texttt{descend}              & geometric & 0  & 11 & --- \\
\midrule
\textbf{Total classical} & & \textbf{76}  & \textbf{0}  & \\
\textbf{Total geometric} & & \textbf{34}  & \textbf{90} & \\
\textbf{Grand total}     & & \textbf{110} & \textbf{90} & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The overall geometric fraction is $34/110 = 0.3091$ (30.91\%).
Among geometric moves, \texttt{add\_obstruction} and
\texttt{resolve\_obstruction} each account for 14 applied
instances, and \texttt{merge} accounts for 6.
The moves \texttt{transport\_along}, \texttt{repair}, and
\texttt{descend} were attempted but failed precondition checks
(65, 14, and 11 failures respectively), indicating that these
sophisticated geometric operations require richer site structure
than our current test programs provide.

