Two observations emerge.  First, programs with multiple judgments
(08\_class with 3 judgments and 10\_complex with 3 judgments) exhibit
higher geometric fractions (33.33\% vs.\ 28.57\%) because the
\texttt{merge} move fires when composing judgments across class
methods or nested structures.  Second, the geometric fraction is
bounded below at 28.57\% ($= 2/7$) even for trivial programs,
because \texttt{add\_obstruction} and \texttt{resolve\_obstruction}
fire once per judgment as bookkeeping operations.

