All 20 cells are covered (Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage}), confirming that
the 13 move kinds span the full deduction-rule and transition-kind
product space.

\subsection{Comparison with \LEAN{} Tactics}\label{sec:eval-lean}

Table~\ref{tab:lean-comparison} compares semantic moves with typical
\LEAN{} tactic counts from the literature
\cite{deMoura2021Lean4,Avigad2023Hitchhiker}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Comparison with \LEAN{} 4 tactic counts (literature baselines).}
\label{tab:lean-comparison}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{\LEAN{} 4} & \textbf{Semantic Moves} & \textbf{Difference} \\
\midrule
Simple lemma tactics   & 3  & 7  & +4 (geometric overhead) \\
Medium lemma tactics   & 8  & 7--27  & variable \\
Complex lemma tactics  & 18 & 27 & +9 (geometric content) \\
Geometric fraction     & 0.0\% & 30.91\% & +30.91 pp \\
Move kinds             & $\sim$6 (intro, apply, \ldots) & 13 & +7 (geometric) \\
Can manipulate trust   & No  & Yes & --- \\
Can record obstructions& No  & Yes & --- \\
Can perform descent    & No  & Yes & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The key finding is that \LEAN's geometric fraction is exactly 0.0\%:
every \LEAN{} tactic is classical.  Our framework adds 7 geometric
move kinds that address proof-search dimensions entirely absent from
term-rewriting-based systems.

The additional moves in our framework (7 for a simple program vs.\
3 for a simple \LEAN{} lemma) reflect the richer proof state: even
trivial programs require bookkeeping moves
(\texttt{add\_obstruction}, \texttt{resolve\_obstruction}) to
maintain the obstruction presheaf.  For complex programs, the
overhead is offset by the geometric content: the 9 additional moves
for 10\_complex (27 vs.\ 18) include 9 geometric moves that
encode inter-method gluing and obstruction tracking---operations that
would require ad hoc workarounds in a pure \LEAN{} development.

\subsection{Timing}\label{sec:eval-timing}

Build times for the semantic site range from $69.9\,\mu$s
(03\_arithmetic) to $523.4\,\mu$s (08\_class), and move-test times
from $189.7\,\mu$s to $2445.8\,\mu$s.  These microsecond-scale
times confirm that semantic moves add negligible overhead to the
verification pipeline.  The dominant cost is in the geometric moves
with failed preconditions: \texttt{transport\_along} accounts for
65 failed attempts across all programs because the current test
programs lack the rich morphism structure (inter-module imports,
cross-package transports) that would make transport succeed.

\subsection{Failed Move Analysis}\label{sec:eval-failed}

Of the 90 total failed move attempts, the breakdown is:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \texttt{transport\_along}: 65 failures (72.2\%)---precondition
        requires an admissible morphism with defined pushforward, which
        is absent in simple programs.
  \item \texttt{repair}: 14 failures (15.6\%)---precondition requires
        a refinable covering family, which is absent when the site has
        no covers.
  \item \texttt{descend}: 11 failures (12.2\%)---precondition requires
        a compatible family over a cover, which exists only for programs
        with branching or exception handling.
\end{itemize}

These failures are expected and informative: they delineate the
boundary between programs that need only classical reasoning and those
that require the full geometric apparatus.


%% ======================================================================
\section{Comparison with Existing Tactic Systems}\label{sec:comparison}
%% ======================================================================

We now systematically compare the expressiveness of semantic moves with
the three major tactic frameworks.

\subsection{Feature Matrix}\label{sec:feature-matrix}

Table~\ref{tab:feature-matrix} compares \LEAN{} 4, Ltac2 (\Coq),
Meta-\Fstar, and semantic moves across key dimensions.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Feature comparison of tactic frameworks.}
\label{tab:feature-matrix}
\begin{tabular}{l|cccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Feature} & \textbf{\LEAN{} 4} & \textbf{Ltac2} &
  \textbf{Meta-\Fstar} & \textbf{Sem.\ Moves} \\
\midrule
Typed tactic language     & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark \\
Sequential composition    & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark \\
Parallel composition      &            &            &            & \checkmark \\
Backtracking              & \checkmark & \checkmark &            & \checkmark \\
SMT integration           &            &            & \checkmark & \checkmark \\
Trust manipulation        &            &            &            & \checkmark \\
Coordinate-aware          &            &            &            & \checkmark \\
Cover-aware               &            &            &            & \checkmark \\
Obstruction tracking      &            &            &            & \checkmark \\
Sheaf descent             &            &            &            & \checkmark \\
Repair generation         &            &            &            & \checkmark \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Tactic Simulation Analysis}\label{sec:simulation}

Of our 13 move kinds, the 6 classical moves can each be simulated
by existing tactics:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \texttt{compose} $\leftrightarrow$ \LEAN's \texttt{apply} /
        \texttt{exact}
  \item \texttt{split} $\leftrightarrow$ \LEAN's \texttt{split} /
        \texttt{cases}
  \item \texttt{strengthen} $\leftrightarrow$ \LEAN's
        \texttt{assumption} / \texttt{exact}
  \item \texttt{weaken} $\leftrightarrow$ \LEAN's \texttt{sorry} /
        \texttt{admit} (with loss of trust)
  \item \texttt{add\_obligation} $\leftrightarrow$ \LEAN's
        \texttt{have} / \texttt{suffices}
  \item \texttt{discharge\_obligation} $\leftrightarrow$ \LEAN's
        \texttt{exact}
\end{itemize}

The 7 geometric moves cannot be simulated:

\begin{theorem}[Simulation Impossibility]\label{thm:simulation}
The moves \texttt{descend}, \texttt{repair}, and
\texttt{transport\_along} cannot be simulated by any finite
combination of \LEAN{} 4 tactics.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We prove the claim for each move individually.

\emph{descend.}
The descent move requires computing a limit in the category of
judgments over a covering diagram.  \LEAN's tactic state is a single
metavariable context with no notion of covering families or
compatibility isomorphisms.  Since the input data (a compatible family
over a cover) cannot be represented in \LEAN's proof state, no
sequence of \LEAN{} tactics can compute the limit.

\emph{repair.}
The repair move modifies the Grothendieck topology by refining a
covering family.  \LEAN's proof state has no component representing a
topology; the closest analogue, the \texttt{simp} lemma set, is a flat
collection of rewrite rules with no hierarchical covering structure.
Refining a topology requires adding morphisms to the site category and
verifying the stability axiom, operations that lie outside the term
algebra.

\emph{transport\_along.}
Transport requires a pushforward functor $f_*$ along a morphism
$f : c \to c'$ in $\mathcal{C}_P$.  \LEAN's \texttt{rw} and
\texttt{conv} rewrite terms along equalities, not along categorical
morphisms between coordinates.  The morphism $f$ encodes structural
relationships (e.g., ``function $g$ is called within module $M$'')
that have no representation in \LEAN's type theory.  Hence no
combination of \texttt{rw}, \texttt{conv}, \texttt{simp}, or custom
tactics can simulate $f_*$.

The argument extends mutatis mutandis to Ltac2 and Meta-\Fstar,
since their proof-state representations are equally devoid of
site structure.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Discussion}\label{sec:comparison-discussion}

The semantic-move framework is not a replacement for existing tactic
systems but a strict extension.  The 6 classical moves correspond
precisely to the core tactic vocabulary shared by \LEAN, \Coq, and
\Fstar.  The 7 geometric moves address a new dimension of proof search
that arises only when the proof state includes topological structure.

A natural question is whether existing systems could be \emph{extended}
to support geometric moves.  In principle, one could add covering
families and obstructions to \LEAN's \texttt{MetavarContext}, but this
would require modifying the trusted kernel---a significant engineering
and trust burden.  Our approach avoids this by operating in a separate
proof-state category that interfaces with existing solvers via the
source channels of the construction loop (\S\ref{sec:construction}).

The separation has a practical advantage: the classical sub-category of
semantic moves maps directly to \LEAN{} tactic scripts.  A bridge tool
could export the classical portion of a proof as a \LEAN{} tactic proof
and flag the geometric residuals---the moves that require site
structure---as ``geometric gaps'' that the \LEAN{} user must address
manually or via custom automation.  We leave the implementation of such
a bridge to future work.

\paragraph{Expressiveness ordering.}
We can summarize the expressiveness relationship as a strict chain:
\[
  \LEAN \; \equiv_{\mathrm{exp}} \; \text{Ltac2} \; \equiv_{\mathrm{exp}} \;
  \text{Meta-\Fstar} \; \subsetneq_{\mathrm{exp}} \; \text{Semantic Moves}
\]
where $\equiv_{\mathrm{exp}}$ denotes equivalent expressiveness on
classical moves and $\subsetneq_{\mathrm{exp}}$ denotes strict
containment.  The gap is precisely the 7 geometric move kinds.


%% ======================================================================
\section{Related Work and Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
%% ======================================================================

\subsection{Related Work}

\paragraph{Tactic frameworks.}
The \LEAN{} 4 tactic monad \cite{deMoura2021Lean4} and Ltac2
\cite{Pedrot2019Ltac2} are the closest relatives of our work.  Both
provide composable proof-search operations, but neither supports
geometric structure.  Meta-\Fstar{} \cite{MartinezEtAl2019MetaFStar}
adds SMT integration but remains term-based.  Mtac2
\cite{Kaiser2018Mtac2} offers typed meta-programming in Coq with
support for non-backtracking effects, but its state is still a proof
term.  Isabelle's Eisbach \cite{Matichuk2016Eisbach} provides a method
language for proof automation; like Ltac2, it operates on proof terms.

\paragraph{Proof search and automation.}
Hammer-style tools \cite{Blanchette2016Hammers} translate goals to
first-order logic for ATP solvers, but the translation erases all
geometric structure.  Machine-learning-guided proof search
\cite{Polu2020GPTf,Yang2024LeanDojo} selects tactics from a trained
model; our framework could serve as a richer action space for such
models by adding 7 geometric move kinds.

\paragraph{Sheaf-theoretic methods.}
Abramsky et al.\ \cite{Abramsky2011Sheaves} use sheaves to model
contextuality in quantum mechanics; our application to proof search
is, to our knowledge, the first use of Grothendieck topologies for
tactic design.  The judgment geometry series
\cite{JuGeo1Sites,JuGeo2Algebra,JuGeo3Descent,JuGeo4Trust} provides
the mathematical foundation on which semantic moves operate.

\paragraph{Compositional verification.}
Compositional program verification has a long history, from Hoare logic
and rely-guarantee reasoning to separation logic and concurrent abstract
predicates.  These frameworks compose proofs across program boundaries
but do so within a fixed proof calculus.  Semantic moves extend this
tradition by introducing a \emph{topological} composition mechanism---the
covering-family structure of the semantic site---that captures not just
sequential and parallel composition but also hierarchical nesting and
modular overlap.

\paragraph{Proof repair.}
Recent work on proof repair \cite{Ringer2021ProofRepair} addresses the
problem of updating proofs when definitions change.  Our \texttt{repair}
move addresses a different but related problem: modifying the
\emph{site structure} (not the proof term) to enable gluing that was
previously obstructed.  The two notions of repair are complementary and
could be combined in a system that supports both term-level and
site-level repair.

\subsection{Conclusion}

We have introduced semantic moves---typed, composable proof-search
operations that act on the full geometric proof state.  Of the 13
move kinds, 7 are geometric and have no analogue in \LEAN, \Coq, or
\Fstar.  Evaluation on 10 programs shows that 34 of 110 applied
moves (30.91\%) are geometric, confirming that nearly a third of
proof-search activity in judgment geometry lies beyond the reach of
term rewriting.

The adaptive control layer and construction loop provide a principled
orchestration mechanism with termination guarantees.  Future work
includes: (i) extending the evaluation to larger programs with richer
site structure, where \texttt{transport\_along}, \texttt{repair}, and
\texttt{descend} succeed more frequently; (ii) integrating semantic
moves as an action space for machine-learning-guided proof search;
and (iii) implementing a bridge to \LEAN{} 4 that exports classical
move sequences as \LEAN{} tactic scripts while flagging geometric
residuals.

\paragraph{Acknowledgments.}
The author thanks the \JuGeo{} development team for the infrastructure
that made the experiments possible, and the anonymous reviewers for
helpful suggestions on the formal presentation.


